\section{Introduction}

\subsection{History of the perturbed long wave equation}
\subsubsection{Unperturbed long wave equation}
Shallow-water models have long been one of the central topics in fluid mechanics and applied mathematics because they provide effective descriptions of weakly dispersive and weakly nonlinear wave propagation in oceans, rivers, and channels. Among these models, long-wave-type equations derived under appropriate asymptotic scalings play an important role in understanding the formation and interaction of solitary waves, as well as the mechanisms of dispersion and nonlinearity.

The (unperturbed) long wave equation is a representative integrable model in this class and can be obtained from asymptotic reductions of the governing equations for shallow water under long-wave assumptions. In its standard form, it captures the balance between nonlinear steepening and dispersive spreading, and it admits rich exact structures such as soliton solutions and conservation laws. Due to its integrability, the equation can be analyzed by powerful tools including the inverse scattering transform, Hirota's bilinear method, and Bäcklund/Darboux transformations, which provide systematic ways to construct multi-soliton solutions and study their interactions.

In many realistic physical settings, however, ideal assumptions are violated by weak dissipation, external forcing, variable bathymetry, or higher-order effects. These contributions naturally lead to perturbed long wave equations, where small perturbation terms are added to the integrable core. The perturbed models are used to quantify how non-ideal effects modify soliton dynamics (e.g., changes in amplitude, phase shift, radiation, or long-time stability) and to connect mathematical predictions to observations and experiments.

\subsubsection{Perturbed long wave equation}
The perturbed long wave equation is typically written as the integrable long wave equation supplemented by small correction terms representing physical effects such as damping, viscosity, wind forcing, or inhomogeneity. Depending on the perturbation structure, the resulting system may lose complete integrability, and one often resorts to perturbation theory for solitons, asymptotic methods, or numerical simulations. Nevertheless, many analytical approaches remain effective, including adiabatic perturbation theory, near-identity transformations, and bilinear-perturbation techniques, which can still yield accurate descriptions of wave evolution over long distances and times.
```

