\section{Computation with Logic as Operator Elimination}
\label{sec-intro}

We pursue an approach to computation with logic emerging from three theses:

\introblock{1. Classical first-order logic as a programming metalanguage.}
Classical first-order logic (FOL) can be used not merely to specify what a computation should satisfy, but as a \emph{metalanguage} in which computations are expressed and transformed. In particular, problem statements, program fragments, and correctness conditions can be written uniformly as formulas, so that program construction becomes formula manipulation.

\introblock{2. Computation as elimination of non-logical operators.}
Many forms of computation can be cast as an \emph{operator-elimination} task: given a formula containing certain operators (e.g., quantifiers, set-theoretic constructors, inductive definitions, aggregates, or domain-specific primitives), compute an equivalent formula in a simpler fragment where those operators no longer occur. The result is an explicit, executable description of the input--output relation, often in a normal form suitable for evaluation.

\introblock{3. Operator elimination unifies algorithm design and correctness.}
Elimination procedures provide both an algorithm and its justification. When an elimination step is sound and preserves equivalence (or yields a controlled approximation), the transformation sequence simultaneously constructs the program and constitutes a correctness argument. This yields a workflow where derivation, optimization, and verification are aspects of the same symbolic process.

The central methodological claim of this paper is that a wide range of computational tasks---from decision procedures and symbolic evaluation to synthesis of executable constraints---can be understood as instances of operator elimination in logic. We develop this view through a collection of elimination principles, illustrate them on representative examples, and discuss how they support practical computation.
